@ Index:   Interactive periodic table of the 118 elements
@ Author:  Victor Valdes
@ Version: 1.2.0
@ Source:  https://github.com/valdesvj/elements47
@ Tested:  in the C47 firmware (T47, sources of 8 Oct 2026): the whole table both ways with the detail box
@          opened and closed at every cell, every screen pixel for pixel the same as the simulator; the
@          stack kept and F1 F2 F3 on 4 and 8 levels (SSIZE4, SSIZE8); v1.1.0 run on a C47 by the author
@ Input:   none (XEQ 'ELEM47')
@ Output:  the periodic table on the screen; key 0 ends with the stack as it was; in the detail box
@          F1 F2 F3 end with Z, the standard atomic weight or the boiling point (K) pushed on the stack
@
@ ---- What it is ----
@ Elements 47 is an interactive periodic table of the 118 elements for the C47 / R47. The table fills the screen
@ (periods 1-7, then the lanthanides and the actinides), with the symbol and Z in each cell; the element under the
@ cursor is named above the table with its Z and standard atomic weight. A detail box shows its symbol, mass, state,
@ boiling point, group and electron configuration, and can hand a value back to your stack.
@
@ ---- How to use it ----
@ Load ELEM47 (the file holds three programs: ELEM47, ELD1 and ELD2; all three are needed) and press XEQ ELEM47.
@   8 4 6 2   move the cursor up, left, right, down (empty cells are skipped; at an edge it comes round the other side)
@   5         open the detail box of the element; in the box 8 4 6 2 move on to the next element, 5 closes it
@   F1 F2 F3  in the box: end the program with Z, the standard atomic weight or the boiling point (K) pushed on
@             your stack (a value shown as "-" is not pushed)
@   0         the end
@ Data: standard atomic weights CIAAW 2024 (abridged, as the IUPAC periodic table); state, boiling point and
@ electron configuration from the PubChem periodic table.
@
@ ---- What it changes on your calculator ----
@ Your stack (4 or 8 levels) and LASTx are given back at the end; with F1-F3 the value is pushed on top, as a number
@ keyed in. ELEM47 uses the registers R20-R56 and a matrix PT while it runs, and clears R20-R56 and deletes PT at the
@ end; the other registers are yours. GRMOD 0 and GRFNT 20 are set at the end.
@
@ ---- Programs and labels ----
@ Three programs with one global label each: ELEM47 (the table and the keys) and ELD1, ELD2 (the element records,
@ Z 1-60 and 61-118, one local label each, read with XEQ IND). Every routine is a local label. Written with the STRUCT
@ commands (IF / ELSE / ENDIF, DO / WHILE / ENDDO, REPEAT / UNTIL: no GTO), AGRAPH, PIXEL and ATEXT; it needs a C47 /
@ R47 firmware with them (00.109.05.00a0.ALPHA of 5 Oct 2026 or later).
@
@ ---- Technical notes ----
@ The 118 elements in an 18 x 9 grid (periods 1-7, then the lanthanides and the actinides), the symbol
@ and Z in each cell, the selected element (name, Z, standard atomic weight) in the empty part of periods 1-3.
@ Keys: 8 4 6 2 move the cursor (empty cells skipped; at an edge around to the other side), 5 the detail
@ box (symbol, mass, state, boiling point, group, electron configuration; 8 4 6 2 move in it, 5 closes it,
@ F1 F2 F3 end the program with Z, the mass or the boiling point in X: a value shown as "-" is not pushed),
@ 0 the end.
@
@ Three programs: ELEM47 (the code) and ELD1, ELD2 (the element records, Z 1-60 and 61-118).
@ C47 / R47 only: written with the STRUCT commands (IF / ELSE / ENDIF, DO / WHILE / ENDDO, REPEAT / UNTIL,
@ AN0007); a build without OPTION_STRUCTURED_PGM does not run it. Original DM42: packages 1-3 only (its STRUCT
@ limit, 10 IF a program: ELEM47 has 10), slower, not tried on one yet; package 4 has no STRUCT. Draws with AGRAPH and ATEXT (GRFNT 21
@ and 10), restores GRMOD 0 and GRFNT 20 at the end.
@ Style: no GTO in any of the three programs; every decision and loop is a STRUCT (the key loop and the key
@ wait are DO / WHILE / ENDDO), and a test before one step remains only where it guards a single XEQ or
@ RTN. No tail calls or jumps between routines; two routines have a second entry label that the first runs
@ into (the cursor runs into the cursor XOR, the detail box into the box from a record already read). The labels are the
@ routines (XEQ) and the data (XEQ IND).
@ The horizontal lines of the table are PIXEL with Y < 0 (one full screen row, one step each), the parts
@ without cells cleared again (AGRAPH in GRMOD 2, ATEXT of spaces in GRMOD 1): about 30 % faster than drawing
@ them cell by cell. Each cell draws only its left edge.
@ Speed: the drawing is unrolled where it runs most: 21 AGRAPH for the cursor, 37 per round of the loop
@ for the box lines (a loop costs about 3 steps per AGRAPH); the routines of the key loop come first in the
@ program (each RTN counts the steps from the top).
@ The user's stack is kept: at the start LocR 10 and X..T (A..D on 8 levels) and LASTx in R.00-R.08, one
@ STO and one R↓ each, 8 times (on 4 levels R↓ goes round twice: no test of the stack size); at the end 8 RCL
@ (and STO L) give them back, then the value of F1-F3 (R.09, local flag F.00) is pushed: the top level goes,
@ as for any number keyed in. The local registers are released at the end (RTN).
@ ELEM47 uses R20-R56 and the matrix PT, and clears R20-R56 (R-CLR) and deletes PT at the end; the other
@ registers are the user's.
@ Data: standard atomic weights CIAAW 2024 (abridged, as the IUPAC periodic table; "-" where there is none),
@ state, boiling point and configuration from the PubChem periodic table.
@
LBL "ELEM47"            ; ELEM47 - the start (the narrative above says what it does and how to use it)
@ the user's stack in the local registers of this level (the main part: no XEQ level reaches them):
@ X..T, A..D one by one with R↓; 8 R↓ give the stack back on 4 levels (twice round) as on 8. Then LASTx
LocR 10
STO R.00
R↓
STO R.01
R↓
STO R.02
R↓
STO R.03
R↓
STO R.04
R↓
STO R.05
R↓
STO R.06
R↓
STO R.07
R↓
LASTx
STO R.08
@ the main part first: the key loop calls the routines of a move and of the box, and each RTN goes back by
@ counting the steps from the start of the program
XEQ 12
@ ---- keys: 8 up, 2 down, 4 left, 6 right, 5 the detail box, 0 the end; other keys ignored ----
DO 01
@   wait for a key: PAUSE 50, KEY? 33 (true: no key yet), as Almanac 47
  DO 02
    PAUSE 50
    KEY? 33
  WHILE 02
  ENDDO 02
@   F1-F3 with the box open: the value in R49, R33 = 82 (the end)
  XEQ 02
  RCL 33
  82
  X≠Y?
WHILE 01
@   the direction of a move from the key code k: R41 = k div 10 - 6 rows, R42 = k mod 10 - 3 columns
@   (8: 53 -> -1 0, 2: 73 -> 1 0, 4: 62 -> 0 -1, 6: 64 -> 0 1); |R41| + |R42| = 1 for these four keys only
  RCL 33
  10
  ÷
  IP
  6
  -
  STO 41
  RCL 33
  10
  MOD
  3
  -
  STO 42
@   a move: cursor off (XOR), the new cell, cursor on, the panel; the detail box open (R36): its new
@   element, from the record the panel has just read
  ABS
  RCL 41
  ABS
  +
  1
  X=Y?
  IF 01
    XEQ 04
    XEQ 19
    XEQ 03
    XEQ 05
    RCL 36
    X≠0?
    XEQ 10
  ENDIF 01
@   5: the detail box (R36 = 1 while it is open); 5 again: the table again (LBL :CLOSE: draws only what
@   the box covered). The arrows with the box open: the cursor, the panel and the box of the new element
  RCL 33
  63
  X=Y?
  IF 02
    RCL 36
    X≠0?
    IF 03
      0
      STO 36
      XEQ 07
    ELSE 03
      1
      STO 36
      XEQ 09
    ENDIF 03
  ENDIF 02
ENDDO 01
@ ---- 0 (or F1-F3): the end: the value of F1-F3 (R49, 0: none) to R.09 and the local flag F.00, R20-R56
@ cleared, GRMOD 0, GRFNT 20, PT deleted, the screen; then the user's stack and LASTx back, and the value
@ pushed on it (the top level goes, as for any number keyed in). RTN releases the local registers ----
RCL 49
STO R.09
X≠0?
SF F.00
20.37
R-CLR
0
GRMOD
20
GRFNT
DELITM "PT"
CLLCD
RCL R.08
STO L
RCL R.07
RCL R.06
RCL R.05
RCL R.04
RCL R.03
RCL R.02
RCL R.01
RCL R.00
FS? F.00
RCL R.09
RTN
@ ---- LBL :FKEY: F1 F2 F3 (codes 11-13) with the box open: R33 = 82 (the end), R49 = Z, the mass or the
@ boiling point (LBL :TONUM:; 0 for "-"). Tests before single steps only (the original DM42: 10 IF) ----
LBL 02
RCL 36
X=0?
RTN
RCL 33
10
-
X≤0?
RTN
3
X<Y?
RTN
R↓
STO 41
82
STO 33
RCL 30
STO 49
RCL 41
1
X=Y?
RTN
